# Build-context exclusions for infra/Dockerfile.
#
# Named `dockerignore`, not `.dockerignore`, and copied into place by whoever
# builds:
#
#   cp infra/dockerignore .dockerignore
#
# Two reasons. Docker only reads `.dockerignore` from the context ROOT, and
# this context is the repo root — so the file cannot simply live in infra/.
# And a permanent root `.dockerignore` would be read by every other docker
# build in the repo (Dockerfile.ci has its own `dockerignore-ci` for exactly
# this reason — same convention, same trick).
#
# Getting this wrong is not subtle: without it the context includes a warm
# `target/` — gigabytes — and the upload to fly's remote builder becomes the
# slowest part of the deploy.

# The build tree, except the one thing the image needs from it: the Studio
# web bundle that CI stages before the build. Docker (unlike git) re-includes
# paths under an excluded directory, so this exception actually works.
target/*
!target/pages
target/pages/*
!target/pages/studio

# History, worktrees, and editor droppings.
.git
.github
.claude
**/*.swp
**/*~

# Large trees no server build reads.
docs-archive
scratch
test_round

# Node/dx caches, if a local build left any behind.
**/node_modules
.dx

# ⚠️ Deliberately short. Cargo needs EVERY workspace member's manifest to
# resolve the lockfile, even for `cargo build -p lp-cloud-server`, and the
# member list is not confined to the obvious directories — `spikes/
# naga-wasm-poc` is a member, and `third_party/{naga,pp-rs,esp-storage,
# ser-write-json}` are path dependencies. Excluding a directory that later
# grows a member breaks the deploy with a "failed to load manifest" ten
# minutes into a remote build. The source tree is tens of megabytes; `target/`
# was the only thing worth excluding, so that is what this file excludes.
